The combined condition treated ATOMIC_WRITE the same as RDMA WRITE during length validation. For a malicious zero-length ATOMIC_WRITE, `resid` and `pktlen` are both 0, so the check `pktlen != resid` passes. The request is accepted, and `atomic_write_reply()` later unconditionally reads 8 bytes from `payload_addr(pkt)`, reading past the logical end of the packet into kernel tailroom and leaking data into the attacker's MR.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (pkt->mask & RXE_ATOMIC_WRITE_MASK) {
    if (resid != sizeof(u64) || pktlen != sizeof(u64) || bth_pad(pkt)) {
        state = RESPST_ERR_LENGTH;
        goto err;
    }
} else if (pkt->mask & RXE_WRITE_MASK) {
    ...
}
Reason:
The fix isolates ATOMIC_WRITE into its own validation block and requires that both `resid` and `pktlen` are exactly 8 bytes (`sizeof(u64)`) and that no padding is present. Any deviation causes the responder to reject the request with a length error before reaching the unconditional 8-byte dereference, preventing the out-of-bounds read.

